{{see also|Florida's 7th congressional district}}
Democrat [[Stephanie Murphy]], who had represented the district since 2017, was re-elected with 55% of the vote in 2020.<ref name=":0" /> Murphy initially stated she would run for re-election, but on December 20, 2021, she announced she would not run.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=https://www.politico.com/news/2021/12/20/murphy-a-leader-of-house-dem-centrists-wont-seek-reelection-525750 |title=Murphy, a leader of House Dem centrists, won't seek reelection |date=December 20, 2021 |work=Politico|author=Ferris, Sarah}}</ref>

The boundaries of the district were redrawn from 2020, determined by the [[2020 United States redistricting cycle|2020 redistricting cycle]]. The 7th district was drawn to be much more conservative than on the previous map, simply by pairing Seminole County with Volusia instead of Orange.

This district was included on the list of Democratic-held seats the [[National Republican Congressional Committee]] was targeting in 2022.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|title=These 47 House Democrats are on the GOP's target list for 2022|website=Roll Call|author=Stephanie Akin|date=February 10, 2021|url=https://www.rollcall.com/2021/02/10/these-47-house-democrats-are-on-the-gops-target-list-for-2022/}}</ref>

